      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the classic exam trap. Pure water and salt water look identical. Both are clear and uniform. But uniform only tells you a mixture is homogeneous. It doesn't tell you a sample is pure. The salt water is still two substances, and you can make it saltier or less salty. Alloys are another trap. Gold-coloured Australian coins are mostly copper, with some aluminium and nickel. Those atoms are spread evenly, but the proportions are chosen by the maker, not fixed by a formula. So an alloy is a homogeneous mixture, not a compound.",
