      "pronunciationText": "Carbon dioxide is a compound. A compound is a substance made of two or more elements, chemically bonded together in a fixed ratio. Every carbon dioxide particle has one carbon atom bonded to two oxygen atoms, so the ratio is always one to two. Water is a compound too, always two hydrogen atoms to one oxygen. Some compounds, like table salt, aren't made of separate molecules at all. Charged particles called ions, sodium and chloride, are packed in a lattice, always one to one. That fixed ratio is what marks a compound. And because its atoms are bonded, you can only split a compound into its elements with a chemical reaction.",
